update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('fe20e582-d97b-4f70-8f45-7289d1888aea'::uuid,'This answer precisely identifies the argument''s flaw. The council president dismisses the proposed ordinance entirely because of who proposed it — Smith, a former bait-and-switch practitioner — rather than because of any problem with the ordinance itself. The merits of a proposal are logically independent of the personal history of its author. Smith''s past use of bait-and-switch might make him a hypocrite, but it says nothing about whether the ordinance is well-crafted, enforceable, or beneficial to consumers. By attacking the source rather than the content, the president avoids engaging with the substance of the proposal entirely. This is a classic ad hominem fallacy: the argument treats biographical information about the proposer as grounds for rejecting the proposition.','This is the prediction almost word for word. The "source" is Smith, the person who proposed the ordinance, and the president''s only reason is Smith''s old store. The "content" is what the ordinance would actually do, which the president never discusses. Test it: could the ordinance still be a good law even though Smith once used bait-and-switch? Yes. So the president''s reason never touches the ordinance. When every reason for rejecting an idea is about the person behind it, look for this source-versus-content wording.'),
('21daac46-1951-4f64-b320-b945b4ac83dc'::uuid,'This answer describes using a single incompatible fact to dismiss a claim as false. But the argument does not work that way. The president is not identifying a fact that contradicts the ordinance and using it to prove the ordinance is wrong. Instead, the president is using a fact about Smith''s personal history to dismiss the ordinance without evaluating it. The distinction matters: the incompatibility described in this answer would be between evidence and the claim being evaluated, but here the "evidence" (Smith''s history) has no logical bearing on the claim (the ordinance''s merit).

Even if an argument did use a single incompatible fact to reject a claim, that is not inherently fallacious. A single counterexample can legitimately refute a universal claim. The flaw described here — relying on one piece of contradictory evidence — is a valid form of reasoning in many contexts, not a logical error. The actual flaw in the argument is ad hominem reasoning, not insufficient evidence against the proposal.','A single fact that truly contradicts a claim does show the claim is false, so this often isn''t even a flaw. And the president never says the ordinance is false. Smith''s old store is not incompatible with the ordinance being a good law; it is a fact about the person who wrote it.'),
('c37e9689-cc6e-4dc1-95a0-005311fe4061'::uuid,'This answer suggests the argument fails to distinguish between deceptive and legitimate sales tactics. While such a distinction could theoretically be relevant to a discussion about regulating sales practices, it does not describe the actual flaw in this argument. The president never discusses the substance of sales tactics at all — the president never addresses what the ordinance would prohibit or whether bait-and-switch is appropriately classified. The entire argument operates at the level of "who proposed it" rather than "what it proposes." The fundamental flaw is the ad hominem attack on Smith, not a failure to make nuanced distinctions about different types of sales tactics.','The president never discusses sales tactics at all, deceptive or legitimate. Nothing in the argument depends on what counts as bait-and-switch, so no distinction between kinds of tactics is needed. The argument fails because it looks only at who proposed the ordinance.'),
('1aaa836f-8659-4cb1-8e78-54f6fba46fe0'::uuid,'Circular reasoning occurs when the conclusion simply restates one of its own premises — when the argument assumes what it is trying to prove. For this to apply, the president''s conclusion (the ordinance does not merit consideration) would need to be the same as one of the premises. But the premise is that Smith used bait-and-switch four years ago, and the conclusion is that his ordinance should be dismissed. These are different claims. The argument may be invalid (because the premise is irrelevant to the conclusion), but it is not circular — it does not assume its own conclusion. Circular arguments at least have the right kind of connection between premise and conclusion; this argument''s problem is the wrong kind of connection entirely.','Circular reasoning would mean the premise already states the conclusion. The premise is about Smith''s store four years ago; the conclusion is that the ordinance does not merit consideration. Those are different claims. The premise is about the wrong thing, but it doesn''t repeat the conclusion.'),
('04933fd5-a80b-4a9e-9426-74683bb5986e'::uuid,'A sampling or generalization flaw occurs when a conclusion about a broader category is drawn from an unrepresentative or insufficient sample. The president is not generalizing from instances at all. The argument does not say "Smith''s store used bait-and-switch and was bad, therefore all bait-and-switch is bad" or "one case proves a pattern." The president''s reasoning is entirely about Smith as an individual and his relationship to the ordinance he proposed. The flaw is about the type of evidence used (biographical rather than substantive), not about the quantity of instances examined. No sampling, surveying, or generalizing is involved in the argument.','There is no generalization here. The president moves from one fact about Smith to one conclusion about Smith''s ordinance and never extends the point to other councilmembers, other ordinances, or bait-and-switch in general. With no sample and no broader category, a generalization flaw has nothing to describe.'),
('ed3f23fc-7335-48a5-ab29-cb6c56562dab'::uuid,'Circular reasoning occurs when a premise is essentially a restatement of the conclusion — the argument assumes what it is trying to prove. That is not happening here. The premise that great art evokes passionate responses is not the same as the conclusion that Reilly''s art is great. The premise about Reilly''s work evoking emotional responses is also not a restatement of the conclusion. These are distinct claims. The argument''s problem is that it reverses the conditional, not that it assumes its own conclusion.','Circular reasoning would mean a premise already assumes Reilly''s art is great. Neither premise does. One is a general rule about great art; the other reports how viewers react to Reilly''s work. The conclusion adds something new. The trouble is the direction the rule is applied in, not a premise that assumes the answer.'),
('9f62d0be-f013-44f2-9602-36d8d2ad58ff'::uuid,'This precisely describes the argument''s flaw. The first premise establishes that evoking passionate responses is a necessary condition for being great art — all great art has this quality. The argument then treats this necessary condition as if it were also sufficient — as if having this quality guarantees greatness. But many things that are not great art could evoke passionate responses. Provocative, disturbing, or even terrible art can elicit intense emotions. The argument assumes that because Reilly''s art satisfies a necessary condition for greatness, it must therefore be great. This is the formal fallacy of affirming the consequent, equivalently described as confusing necessary and sufficient conditions.','This is the prediction in formal language. "A condition that is necessary for having a certain quality" is evoking passionate responses, which every great work must do. "As if it must also be sufficient" is the author''s move: Reilly''s work has the passionate response, so it must be great. Check it by plugging in the terms: did the author treat a passionate response as enough for greatness? Yes, that is the whole argument. Whenever a Flaw argument gives you "if X, then Y," shows Y, and concludes X, look for this answer.'),
('c264dadb-e5fd-40a0-9fc4-26d2b5811dda'::uuid,'This answer suggests the argument misapplies a general claim to an atypical instance. But there is no reason to believe Reilly''s work is atypical of the class being discussed. The argument applies the claim "great art evokes passion" to Reilly''s work, which does evoke passion. The problem is not that Reilly''s work is an unusual case that does not fit the general claim — it is that the argument reverses the direction of the conditional. Even if Reilly''s work were perfectly typical, the argument would still be flawed.','For C to fit, Reilly''s work would need to be an unusual case that the general claim doesn''t really cover. Nothing suggests that. The rule about great art isn''t misapplied to an odd case; it is run backward. Even if Reilly were a perfectly typical painter, the reversal would still be a flaw.'),
('d107ecbb-6eba-4813-a22c-25320516fdc7'::uuid,'This answer describes a hasty generalization — drawing a broad conclusion from too few instances. The argument is not making a generalization at all. It is making a specific claim about one artist''s work (Reilly''s art is great). There is no indication that the argument uses an insufficient number of Reilly''s works or an insufficient number of viewer reactions. The flaw is structural — reversing a conditional — not statistical.','A hasty generalization builds a broad claim from a small sample. The argument''s general claim, that great works evoke passionate responses, is offered as a premise, not drawn from examples. The conclusion is about one painter''s work. No sample, no generalization to criticize.'),
('63816d8a-5592-4f98-9776-424061a296a8'::uuid,'This answer claims the argument draws a value judgment from non-value premises. But the first premise is itself a value statement: "great works of art evoke passionate responses" contains the value-laden term "great." The conclusion ("his art is great") and the premise both involve the concept of artistic quality. So the argument does not lack value-laden premises — it has one. The flaw is not about moving from factual premises to value conclusions; it is about reversing the direction of a conditional relationship within those value claims.','Check the premises for value language. The first premise is about great works of art, and "great" is the same value judgment the conclusion makes about Reilly. So the argument does start from a premise involving values. E describes a gap this argument does not have.'),
('3f57b890-d9c3-440f-8bac-4b262ff5ad2d'::uuid,'Points out an advantage answering machines have over voicemail.','This is the missing advantage we predicted. With an answering machine, you can hear who is calling and then decide whether to pick up. Voice mail can''t do that. Anyone who likes screening calls now has a reason to keep a machine even if voice mail is cheaper and more convenient, so the machines may not become obsolete. When an argument predicts that a new option will replace an old one, look for a benefit the old option keeps that the new one lacks.'),
('b465e72d-0902-4d8a-8703-0ed208525611'::uuid,'This is the most popular wrong answer. The passage ties centralization to deaths *in a collapse*, not to a greater need for food. Food production is what drives centralization, not the other way around.','The passage links centralization to one thing: how many people perish when a society collapses. It never says centralized societies need more food. The link it does give runs the other way: the push for more food is the reason to centralize. A reverses that, making centralization the cause of a greater need for food.'),
('9ad6805f-2308-4a22-a1e5-2f6a4117aead'::uuid,'Centralizing for more food makes collapses worse, so technology plainly doesn''t fix every collapse problem. "Not every" asks for very little.','B asks for very little. "Not every" problem means at least one problem survives the technology. The passage supplies one: the technology route to more food requires centralization, and centralization makes collapse deadlier. Technology doesn''t prevent that higher death toll; the author says the effort makes it worse. When an answer is this modestly worded, check whether the stimulus gives you one clear case. Here it does.'),
('95d2ac70-01e5-4301-bab1-276df99e9c34'::uuid,'Population growth continuing "indefinitely" is far beyond anything here.','The passage says populations will increase, but nothing about the rate of growth, let alone a rate that keeps rising forever. "Continue to increase indefinitely" is a claim about the future that no statement here supports.'),
('dce8601f-8057-4639-996a-c9a8db1a766b'::uuid,'"Only by technology" is never claimed.','The passage says technology will enable food production to increase. It never says technology is the only way to do it. If anything, it stresses centralization as a requirement for meaningful increases. To support "only by improved technology," we would need to rule out every other route, and nothing does.'),
('d2087976-f065-4a03-9d00-53af8db03539'::uuid,'The passage gives no history of centralization rising with technology. It talks about what *would* happen.','The passage''s history covers one relationship: more centralized societies lost a greater share of their people when they collapsed. It never says societies became more centralized as technology improved. The centralization it discusses is something societies would have to undertake, not a trend it reports.'),
('88fa80c1-6a8d-4f41-a4e2-06bc9b93e03e'::uuid,'The author is not using one Egyptian cup to generalize about what most Egyptians did. The cup establishes an instance of alcoholic beer in Egypt by 2000 B.C.; the issue is whether that instance was the world''s first.','A generalization flaw would need a claim about Egyptian society as a whole, such as how common beer drinking was. The conclusion isn''t about that. One cup is enough to show alcohol was being made in Egypt by 2000 B.C. The real question is whether anyone made it before then.'),
('f0375ec8-e3e1-4fef-ba3d-2bfba1f62a19'::uuid,'"Alcoholic beverage" includes wine and beer in both the evidence and the conclusion. The argument compares dates, not two meanings of the term.','The term holds steady. The premises mention Babylonian wine and Egyptian beer, and the conclusion''s "alcoholic beverages" covers both in the same sense. Nothing shifts meaning between premises and conclusion. The argument compares dates, so the weak spot is about what came first, not what the words mean.'),
('3de5d70d-4fdc-4b17-9043-13b657a42b57'::uuid,'The author does not say Babylonian wine production depended on Egyptian beer production. Being earlier than one known Babylonian example is offered as evidence for priority, not for transmission of technology.','The argument never says Babylonian wine-making depended on Egyptian brewing. It claims only that Egypt came first in time. C describes a claim about one society''s technology depending on another''s, and the author makes no such claim, so there is nothing for this criticism to attack.'),
('4a8201c9-e781-4de8-94ba-6bcce841130c'::uuid,'The cup is older than the cited Babylonian evidence, so Egypt has the earlier *known* example of the two. Another society might have made alcohol even earlier without leaving evidence we have found. The oldest discovered instance need not be the first historical instance.','This is the gap we predicted. "The first known instance of a kind" is the 2000 B.C. Egyptian cup, the oldest evidence found so far. "The first instance of that kind" is what the conclusion claims: the Egyptians were the first society ever to produce alcohol. The author treats the two as the same. Test it: could some society have made alcohol before 2000 B.C. without leaving evidence we have found? Yes, and the Babylonians losing their title shows new finds can overturn an old first. When a Flaw conclusion claims a first, look for this answer.'),
('81ca6f1f-1b28-4e5e-a272-c34f0e0e98a1'::uuid,'The author supplies the 1500 B.C. Babylonian wine date as background. Even if that date were well documented, the Egyptian cup would not establish that no society produced alcohol before Egypt.','Arguments may state background facts without proving them, and the 1500 B.C. date mainly tells us what used to be believed. Supporting it wouldn''t help the argument anyway: even a well-documented Babylonian date can''t show that no society produced alcohol before 2000 B.C. The flaw is elsewhere.'),
('a8162afd-26ef-4db8-ae4d-e24315bdde22'::uuid,'This negates the assumption of the argument that if a person''s promise is selfishly motivated then that promise is unreliable.','The argument assumes that a selfishly motivated promise is unreliable. A negates both halves of that rule: a promise that is not selfishly motivated is reliable. That is a different conditional, and the argument never needs it. It says nothing about unselfish promises at all, so it isn''t presuming anything about them.'),
('9621931d-ac06-4316-ba83-092ba343f2e5'::uuid,'The argument does not go so far as to say that such promises are never kept, but rather says that they are unreliable.','"Never kept" is far stronger than "unreliable." An unreliable promise is one we can''t count on; it might still be kept some of the time. The argument only needs selfish promises to be untrustworthy, not broken every time, so it doesn''t presume what B describes.'),
('82f4a5c5-d965-4586-9952-b263dfe274c8'::uuid,'This describes a causal reasoning error: confusing an effect with its cause. That flaw isn''t in this argument, which moves from a selfish motive to the claim that the promises are unreliable.','Confusing cause and effect would mean treating a result as if it were the reason. The argument doesn''t do that. It names the candidates'' purpose, getting elected, as the motive behind the speeches, which is exactly what a purpose is. The flaw is the jump from that motive to unreliable promises.'),
('22db832a-30a1-4d0c-8c23-319b3eb9a985'::uuid,'This correctly describes the difference between a questionable source and an unreliable claim.','This matches the prediction. The "ulterior motive" is the candidates'' purpose of getting elected, and "a promise need not be unreliable" denies the second half of the conclusion. The argument moves from why a promise was made to whether it can be trusted, and D points out that the first doesn''t settle the second. Whenever an argument rejects a claim because of the speaker''s motives, look for an answer that separates the motive from the merit of what was said.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and t.body=v.ob returning t.id;